% ============================================================================
% mitosis-phases — each phase of mitosis and the defining event that happens
% in it (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% One of the first big ideas of cell biology, and a rung a MEDICINE or genetics
% agent reasons UP from: a cell does not split all at once — mitosis proceeds
% through FOUR ordered phases, and each phase is defined by one characteristic
% event. In prophase the loose chromatin is transformed into visible
% chromosomes; in metaphase those chromosomes line up down the middle of the
% cell; in anaphase the sister chromatids are pulled apart, one to each future
% daughter cell; in telophase a new nuclear membrane forms around each set.
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? mitosis_phase(metaphase, $Event)   % chromosomes_line_up
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `mitosis_phase(phase, event)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the event
% atom WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a
% word that is not one of the four mitotic phases (it never invents an event).
%
% The four phases IN ORDER, with the defining event the source states, as a
% little truth table (the ordering — prophase → metaphase → anaphase →
% telophase — is itself asserted by the source's "The four phases of mitosis
% are ..." list; the table stores the pairs, and the ordinal reading is the
% top-to-bottom row order):
%
%     phase       defining event          a beginner's cue
%     ---------   ---------------------   -----------------------------------
%     prophase    chromatin_forms_        chromatin is transformed INTO
%                 chromosomes             visible chromosomes (chromatids)
%     metaphase   chromosomes_line_up     paired chromosomes line up between
%                                         the centrioles (the cell's middle)
%     anaphase    chromatids_separate     chromatids are pulled apart — one
%                                         from each pair to each daughter cell
%     telophase   nuclear_membrane_forms  a new nuclear membrane forms around
%                                         each set of chromosomes
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind an event and recall its phase:
%
%     ? mitosis_phase($Phase, chromosomes_line_up)   % metaphase
%
% A word that is NOT one of these four phases — `interphase` (the resting phase
% BETWEEN divisions, not a phase OF mitosis), `prometaphase`, `cytokinesis`,
% `rock` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a phase recall ABSTAINS on it rather
% than inventing an event. That honest-abstention property is what makes the
% table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the phase→event pairs
% it can ground, and only those. (Interphase and cytokinesis ARE described on
% the same source page, but as stages OUTSIDE the four-phase mitosis list — so
% they are intentionally excluded here to keep the relation to the four classic
% mitotic phases the source names.)
%
% The `event` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be a
% faithful paraphrase of the event the source EXPLICITLY states for that phase
% — never one the source does not support. Where the everyday word differs from
% the source's word, the atom stays anchored to the source's own span (the
% source says chromatin is "transformed into chromosomes", so the atom is
% `chromatin_forms_chromosomes`, not the textbook shorthand "condense").
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every phase→event pair
% below was read out of a fetched NCI SEER Training Modules "Cell Cycle" page,
% and any pair whose event could not be verified there would have been
% dropped). The single primary source, and the verbatim span that fixes each
% row:
%
%   Source: NCI SEER Training Modules — "Cell Cycle" (U.S. National Cancer
%   Institute, cancer.gov), which states: "The four phases of mitosis are
%   Prophase ... Metaphase ... Anaphase ... Telophase".
%   https://training.seer.cancer.gov/disease/cancer/biology/cycle.html
%
%   prophase → chromatin_forms_chromosomes
%     "Chromatin is transformed into chromosomes composed of pairs of filaments
%      called chromatids (each is a complete genetic copy of its chromosome)."
%
%   metaphase → chromosomes_line_up
%     "Paired chromosomes become lined up between the centrioles."
%
%   anaphase → chromatids_separate
%     "Chromatids are pulled toward the centrioles. One chromatid from each pair
%      goes to each daughter cell."
%
%   telophase → nuclear_membrane_forms
%     "A nuclear membrane forms around each set of chromosomes forming a new
%      nucleus with a nucleolus."
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the SEER statement that fixes the
% first row (prophase → chromatin_forms_chromosomes). Every OTHER row's verbatim
% span is listed above and echoed in an inline `%` comment on the row, so each
% event remains independently checkable against the same primary page. The
% source is the NCI SEER Training Modules, a primary U.S. government (National
% Cancer Institute) teaching resource, so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_adjudication_no_interpretive_gating.)
% ============================================================================

table mitosis_phase {
    columns phase, event

    row (prophase, chromatin_forms_chromosomes)   % "Chromatin is transformed into chromosomes composed of pairs of filaments called chromatids (each is a complete genetic copy of its chromosome)."
    row (metaphase, chromosomes_line_up)          % "Paired chromosomes become lined up between the centrioles."
    row (anaphase, chromatids_separate)           % "Chromatids are pulled toward the centrioles. One chromatid from each pair goes to each daughter cell."
    row (telophase, nuclear_membrane_forms)       % "A nuclear membrane forms around each set of chromosomes forming a new nucleus with a nucleolus."

    source "Chromatin is transformed into chromosomes composed of pairs of filaments called chromatids (each is a complete genetic copy of its chromosome)."
    locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/disease/cancer/biology/cycle.html"
    trust authoritative
}
